.TH VX 6
.SH NAME
vx \- program image formats VECTRA01 and VECTRA02
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B VECTRA01
.br
.B VECTRA02
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.B .vx
file is a program the loader in
.IR user (9)
can run: not an ELF, but the little that a loader needs from one, behind a
four-byte magic that names which of the two shapes follows.
.IR Build (8)
links every program as an ELF and converts it with
.B elf_to_image
before it reaches a
.B .vx
file; nothing else writes one.
.PP
.B VECTRA01
is one page of position-bound code, the shape a program written directly
in the kernel's own tree of blobs takes.
The header is four eight-byte little-endian words:
.IP
.EX
+0   magic      "VECTRA01"
+8   entry      the virtual address of the first instruction
+16  text       how many bytes of code follow the header
+24  reserved   zero, and refused when it is not
.EE
.PP
.I Text
must be no more than one page and no less than one byte, and
.I entry
must fall inside it.
The bytes after the header are mapped read-only and executable at a fixed
address, and the program is given one page of writable data and one page
of stack besides.
Its first argument is the data page's address, which is the whole of what
such a program is handed; there is no
.B abi.Args
record.
.PP
.B VECTRA02
is a linked program's shape: a segment table, one row per contiguous
range of memory the program wants at its own permissions.
The header is the same four words with a different last one:
.IP
.EX
+0   magic      "VECTRA02"
+8   entry      the virtual address of the first instruction
+16  nsegs      how many segment rows follow, 1 to 4
+24  reserved   zero, and refused when it is not
.EE
.PP
Each row is thirty-two bytes, and the rows are followed by each segment's
payload in turn, of
.I filesz
bytes:
.IP
.EX
+0   vaddr      the segment's virtual address, page-aligned
+8   filesz     bytes of payload that follow, at most memsz
+16  memsz      bytes the segment occupies once mapped
+24  flags      bit 0 write, bit 1 execute
.EE
.PP
The loader refuses a table that breaks any of its rules: a segment must
start on a page boundary, no segment may set both the write and the
execute bit, segments must be given in ascending order and must not
overlap, every segment must fall below the stack, and the entry must lie
inside a segment that sets the execute bit.
A segment with fewer file bytes than memory bytes is zero-filled past
.IR filesz ,
which is how a program gets writable memory an ELF's linker put in
.BR .bss .
.PP
A
.B VECTRA02
program is given a stack of its own besides its segments, sixteen pages
growing down from the top of the user half of the address space; only the
top page is backed at first, and the rest are filled in as the stack
grows.
Its first argument is zero in the header, and
.I spawn
and
.I exec
(see
.IR exec (2))
fill it in with the address of an
.B abi.Args
record they build on the fresh stack, which
.IR intro (2)
describes.
.PP
Every segment and the stack together must fit within the frames one
program is allowed, which bounds a
.B VECTRA02
program's whole memory image regardless of how many segments it uses.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/user/image.odin
.br
.B build.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR build (8),
.IR user (9),
.IR intro (2),
.IR exec (2)
.SH BUGS
There is no relocation and no dynamic linking; every address in a segment
table is absolute, fixed by the link script the program was built with.
